Contributing to libdvbs2x_vlsnr
===============================

This is a small C11 library that depends only on the C library and
libm.  The rules below keep the tree consistent.  Please read them
before sending a change.

Everything in the tree is written in English and in ASCII.  Do not add
any non-ASCII byte: no typographic quotes, no long dashes, no accented
letters, no arrows.  Where you might reach for such a character, write
the plain form instead: a hyphen "-", straight quotes, "->" for an
arrow, and "x" for a multiplication sign.


Building and testing
--------------------

	make		build the static library
	make test	build and run the test programs
	make clean	remove the build products

A change must build with no warnings under the flags already set in the
Makefile (-Wall -Wextra -Werror), and "make test" must pass, before you
submit it.


Coding style
------------

Indent with tabs, one tab per level, and treat a tab as eight columns.
Do not indent with spaces.  Spaces are allowed only to align text that
follows the leading tabs, such as the arguments of a call that spills
onto a second line.

Keep a line within 80 columns.  When a statement does not fit, break it
and line the continuation up under the opening parenthesis.

Put the opening brace of a function on its own line.  Everywhere else
the opening brace stays on the line that starts the block, with one
space in front of it:

	int dvbs2x_example(int n)
	{
		if (n < 0) {
			handle();
			return DVBS2X_ERR_PARAM;
		}

		return 0;
	}

The closing brace goes on its own line, in the column of the statement
that opened the block.  Keep "else", and the "while" of a do/while
loop, on the same line as the brace that precedes it.

Leave the braces off a block that holds a single statement.  If one arm
of an if/else needs braces, put braces on both arms.

Put a space after "if", "for", "while", "switch", and "return".  Put no
space between a function name and the "(" that follows it.  Put a space
on each side of a binary operator, and none after a unary operator or a
cast.  Do not pad the inside of "(" or "[".

Bind the "*" of a pointer to the name, not the type: write
"struct foo *bar", never "struct foo* bar".

Name functions, variables, and structure members in lower case with
underscores between words.  Do not use mixed case.  Write macros and
enumeration constants in upper case.  Prefix every name the library
exports with "dvbs2x_".

Name a structure type as "struct thing" and use it that way; do not put
a structure or a pointer behind a typedef.  Use the fixed-width types
from <stdint.h> for data with a defined bit width.  Mark a pointer
parameter "const" when the function does not write through it.

Declare the variables of a block at its top, ahead of the first
statement.

Use /* ... */ for comments.  The // form appears only on the SPDX line
at the very top of a .c file.  A comment that spans lines opens with /*
on its own line and begins each later line with " * ".  Start every
source file with an SPDX license identifier and one line that says what
the file holds.  Comment the reasoning behind code that is not obvious;
do not narrate code that speaks for itself.

Keep a function short and give it one job.  Return 0 (DVBS2X_OK) on
success and a negative DVBS2X_ERR_* code on failure.  On an error path,
jump with "goto" to a single cleanup block at the end of the function
and release resources in the reverse of the order you took them.


Commit messages
---------------

Put one logical change in each commit.  Do not mix unrelated edits, and
do not spread a single change across several commits.

The first line is the summary.  Begin it with "dvbs2x-vlsnr: ", then a
short description in the imperative mood and in lower case, with no full
stop at the end.  Keep the whole line under about 75 columns:

	dvbs2x-vlsnr: return error codes from the public API

Leave the second line blank.  After it write the body: state the
problem first, then what the change does and why.  Use plain
present-tense sentences and wrap them at 72 columns.

Sign every commit.  Commit with "git commit -s", which adds the line:

	Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.com>

The sign-off states that you wrote the change, or otherwise have the
right to submit it under the library's license, GPL-2.0-or-later; this
is the Developer Certificate of Origin.  Add no other trailers unless
they apply.

A full example:

	dvbs2x-vlsnr: fix q_ldpc for the short-frame codes

	MODCODs 5, 7, and 9 reused the normal-frame circulant size.  The
	quasi-cyclic parity-check addressing was then wrong and the codes
	had no coding gain.

	Set q_ldpc to (fec_len - k_ldpc) / 360 for every code so the
	decoder walks the matrix correctly.

	Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.com>
